---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/guides/campaign-restraint';
const title = 'Campaign Restraint for SMS Marketing | Shopify SMS Marketing';
const desc = 'Make every promotional slot answer a customer need. Review total contact pressure, evidence of fatigue and the value of an additional message before expanding the calendar.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} dateModified="2026-11-25" />
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/guides" class="link text-sm">← All SMS guides</a>
      <p class="badge mt-6">Decide whether the next send earns its slot</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">Campaign Restraint for SMS Marketing</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-orange-950/80">{desc}</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify">
<h2>Build a calendar of reasons to send</h2>
<p>Put product releases, requested restocks, useful replenishment reminders and genuine shipping deadlines on the calendar. An empty week does not create a customer need. For each proposed message, write the audience, reason it matters now, last related contact and what changes if you omit it. This makes restraint a reviewable decision rather than a vague instruction to send less.</p>
<p>There is no universal two-to-four-text monthly allowance that proves a program is healthy. A requested stock alert and a repeated sale reminder create different expectations. Start from the capture promise and customer context, choose an internal planning limit, then revise it using your own evidence. Applicable sending restrictions remain separate from that commercial choice.</p>
<h2>Count the messages people can actually receive</h2>
<p>A calendar of broadcasts misses welcome, cart, browse and replenishment flows. Inspect profiles that qualify for several at once. Record attempted, skipped and delivered messages separately; a scheduled campaign is not the same as a delivered touch. Include email where it repeats the same promotion.</p>
<p><a class="link" href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/115002779311">Klaviyo's Smart Sending documentation</a> describes channel-specific windows. This illustrates why a vendor frequency switch needs inspection; it is not evidence that all tools enforce a combined cap. Checked November 25, 2026.</p>
<h2>Use a release decision, not a recurring entitlement</h2>
<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Possible action</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Is this a requested alert with accurate stock?</td><td>Send to the eligible request audience after permission and timing checks</td></tr>
<tr><td>Does this repeat an unchanged offer?</td><td>Omit it or narrow to a relevant audience with a new reason to act</td></tr>
<tr><td>Has a purchaser already completed this task?</td><td>Remove that promotional reminder, retaining appropriate service communication</td></tr>
<tr><td>Is eligibility or inventory data stale?</td><td>Pause the affected send until an owner resolves it</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>For a proposed collector drop, begin with requested access and relevant product interest. A follow-up might be justified by a verified restock or a real access deadline. Do not invent scarcity because the launch message underperformed. Lifetime spend can inform relevance, but a fixed dollar threshold is not a universal definition of loyalty.</p>
<h2>Diagnose fatigue before changing frequency</h2>
<p>Compare opt-outs per delivered message by campaign type and capture cohort, using consistent denominators. Include delivery failures, complaint themes and customer replies. A rise in opt-outs can reflect a mismatch in acquisition promises, irrelevant products, a confusing offer or timing as well as message volume. Review the changed inputs before attributing the problem to creative or frequency alone.</p>
<p>Write a stop rule before launch: name the metric, baseline period, minimum exposure needed to interpret it, person monitoring it and pause action. Select thresholds from your own risk tolerance and history; a borrowed percentage may hide a problem on a small or newly acquired list. Urgent permission or routing failures warrant stopping the affected send without waiting for a statistically useful sample.</p>
<h2>Test the marginal reminder</h2>
<p>A proposed experiment can randomize eligible subscribers between the planned sequence and the same sequence without its final promotional reminder. Keep offer eligibility, other channels and the measurement window consistent. Compare contribution per eligible person and opt-outs, not only revenue per send: fewer sends can improve that ratio while reducing total contribution.</p>
<p>For example, if both groups contain 1,000 eligible people and their net contributions are $600 and $550, the observed difference is $0.05 per eligible person. That is illustrative arithmetic, not an observed lift or proof of causality. Account for uncertainty, returns and exposure from other campaigns before changing the program. Do not repeatedly inspect results and stop only when a favorable number appears.</p>
<h2>Review and carry decisions forward</h2>
<ol><li>List the messages a typical eligible profile received, with campaign and flow identifiers.</li><li>Review which messages added a distinct benefit and which repeated a task already completed.</li><li>Record the decision to retain, narrow, omit or test each recurring slot.</li><li>Assign implementation owners and confirm queued messages reflect the revised plan.</li></ol>
<p>Keep a decision log with the audience definition and dates so a later calendar owner does not restore a discarded reminder without its context. Review again when capture promises, catalog, markets or automated flows change. Use <a class="link" href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">channel coordination</a> for handoffs and <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">pricing economics</a> for costs; the decision here is whether an additional promotional touch earns its place.</p>
  </div></section>
</BaseLayout>
